# JoystickPushTask — Project Plan (condensed 9 Oct 2026)

Head-fixed mouse 1D forelimb object-push task, forked from the Mathis joystick-pull LabVIEW rig. This file is force-tracked (path is in `.gitignore`, but edits still commit). Keep one copy only. Older detailed notes live in git history. Where this file disagrees with older notes, this file wins.

## Safety rules (always)
- Class 3B 635 nm laser on `PFI3` / terminal 42: keep keyed OFF. Never toggle PFI3 blind. Do not touch the laser pulse nodes in the magnet loop.
- Never power the Ledex coil from AO0 (AO0 is only the 0–10 V command; PCIe-6321 AO limit ±5 mA).
- 1 A inline fuse must stay in the 12 V positive lead of the PVA/Ledex supply. Do not use the magnet's reserved 12 V Goobay supply for anything else.
- Normal Stop only; toolbar Abort is emergency-only (skips cleanup frames and the auto timing file).
- Never press an animal's paw onto the pad. Keep `mag = 0` until the unperturbed task is stable.
- Never hand-edit `.vi` binaries or internal names. Do not Git-merge independently edited `.vi` files; the repo copy is the canonical hierarchy.

## Working conventions
- Two machines share this repo through GitHub `master`: the rig PC (Windows, LabVIEW, hardware, data) and a Mac (planning, docs, parameter files, Python scripts, CAD). Pull before editing; commit and push after each finished step. Commits use the `maxchalabi` identity and imperative sentence messages ending in a period; no Cursor co-author trailer (a local commit-msg hook strips it).
- The VIs are edited by hand in LabVIEW on the rig PC, so guide changes one small step at a time (which node, which wire, how to check it) and have the user confirm before moving on. Agents never edit `.vi` binaries. Prefer parameter files, front-panel controls and Python/scripts over diagram changes where possible.
- After every step, update this file with the new status (and keep it short: replace stale items, do not append logs). The repo is public: keep this file free of private chat, people's names and purchasing/admin details.
- Bench-test everything animal-free first; one variable at a time; no improvising circuit changes during an animal session.
- Parameter files must be validated after editing: exact row count, 27 tab-separated columns per row, consistent row definition.

## Rig and DAQ
- NI PCIe-6321, SCB-68A breakout (terminal map in `RIG_INVENTORY.md`). Saved NI-MAX tasks are incomplete; audit inline channels. NI-MAX backup: `ni-max/JoystickPushTask_NIMAX_2026-08-20.nce`.
- Channels:
  - AI task `joysticklickframe_push`, continuous 10 kHz, order `ai1` (Y = push, white, queue idx 0), `ai0` (X = lateral, red, idx 1), `ai2` (rest pad, RSE 0–5 V). `joystick pos` = Y, X, 0, FSR (idx 3, matches the lab DataJoint layout).
  - AO0 `MagnetPush_Dev1` (Ledex via PVA); AO1 `LickSpout_Dev1` (Actuonix L12-30-50-12-I, 0–5 V, own 12 V PSU).
  - DO `Water_Dev1` P0.0; `SuccessCue_Dev1` P0.1 (terminal 17, D GND 15; Adafruit #1536 buzzer).
  - `frame counter_Dev1` on PFI8 (ctr0); pulse out ctr1 on PFI13 at 50 Hz. Camera runs on the rig PC.
  - Rest pad terminals: +5 V terminal 8, `ai2` terminal 65, AI GND terminal 64.
- `avg joystick and frame trig lick3.vi` acquisition loop: Read stays **10** samples (1 kHz logged), input buffer **100000** installed, in-loop `stop in` Value property node deleted, `stop all` global (`PushTask Globals.vi`) drives all loops. Earlier -200279 overrun is provisionally fixed; a 25–30 min no-animal run with rewards, stopped cleanly, is still to be reported. Do not switch Read to 100.
- Do not run two simultaneous AI tasks (one AI timing engine). 1 MΩ source impedance ghosts `ai0` onto `ai2` on this multiplexed ADC.
- Data on the rig: `C:\Users\Mathis_Lab_1\Desktop\RIG1_DATA` (`joystick_`, `REWARD_`, `TRIAL_`, `EVENTS_`, `TIMESTAMPS_LABVIEW_`). Python 3.6.4 at `C:\Users\Mathis_Lab_1\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python36-32\python.exe`.

## Current status (9 Oct 2026)
- **Rest-pad sensor.** Live pad is still the Interlink FSR 402 under tape with a 3.15 mm puck and a 1 MΩ divider; `ai2` unloaded 0.6–0.825 V (follows joystick X), threshold about 1 V. A resting mouse paw does not trigger it (FSR needs ~10–20 gf; a resting forelimb is ~1–2 gf). Hazard: a large X excursion could give a false contact. Resistor/threshold changes cannot fix this (the FSR switches from open circuit at its break force). Until a new sensor is bench-validated, mouse sessions stay on cue-then-water habituation (see Mice).
- **Replacement: capacitive touch, Arduino build (nothing assembled yet).** Capacitive is approved, with grounded shielding required during ephys. SingleTact and FlexiForce are not options (purchase failed; FlexiForce turn-on is not lower than the FSR's). Requirement: ~10×10 mm flat pad, sensitivity knob mountable elsewhere.
  - Design: classic Arduino Nano (ATmega328, A000005; not Nano Every, library compatibility) + 10×10 mm copper-tape pad under thin tape + 10 kΩ linear pot to the screw terminals (A0, 5 V, GND). Sketch reads the pad (CapacitiveSensor-style, ~1 MΩ send resistor), compares with the pot level, outputs push-pull 0/5 V on D2 to `ai2` (high = touch, so the LabVIEW `>` comparison stays; low impedance, no X ghosting). Power from rig +5 V (terminal 8, 5V pin), GND to AI GND 64; unplug USB when running from the rig.
  - Remove the FSR and the 1 MΩ when installing. Pad wire short (<30 cm), shielded wire with shield to GND at the Arduino end only; solder the wire to bare copper tape before sticking it down. Threshold on `ai2` about 2.5 V.
  - Parts (via Galaxus/Digitec, Distrelec as the catalogue fallback): Arduino Nano item 6864826; Nano screw-terminal adapter ASX00037 (Galaxus 23950109, Distrelec 303-68-956); Velleman copper tape item 22201928; Bourns 51AAD-B28-A15L pot (Galaxus 69284715, Distrelec 301-94-343, 6.35 mm slotted shaft, needs knob). Still needed: 6.35 mm knob, Mini-USB cable. If the Galaxus pot or adapter cannot be ordered, use the Distrelec equivalents.
  - Bench checks before mice: finger and mouse-sized contact, wet pad and water drops, licking/spout nearby, joystick and metal nearby, cable movement, long rest (>60 s) stays high, release goes low, joystick sweep does not move `ai2`. Sketch and wiring/calibration guide are written: `arduino/touch_pad/touch_pad.ino` + `README.md` (CapacitiveSensor library, D4 send, 1 MΩ, D6 pad, A0 pot, D2 out; compiles for `arduino:avr:nano:cpu=atmega328`, 23% flash). Not yet run on hardware. Data impact: joystick-file column 4 (`ai2`, `joystick pos` idx 3) becomes a ~0 V / ~5 V touch signal (threshold 2.5 V, a few tens of ms switch lag, no force information, no X ghosting); file layout unchanged. DataJoint side must treat it as Boolean for new sessions and record the sensor type (`fsr402` or `capacitive`) per session. Next: when parts arrive, upload, calibrate with the Serial Plotter, run the bench checklist, then install on the rig.
  - Rejected for good: AT42QT1010 (recalibrates after ~60 s), Omron E2K-F (too big), M12/M18 industrial sensors (too big or expensive), load-cell route (needs amp and mechanics; fallback only).
- **Mice (stay on stage-1 habituation until the new sensor works).** Use only `deliverWater_RIG1_cue.vi` (50 ms cue then water), `Water on time (ms)` 240 (~8 µl), extend spout to 3.0 V before the first reward and retract to 0 V at the end (that VI does not move the spout). Keep the push VI closed. Cap 25 min and the approved water limit; stop when licking fades; tally rewards. Leave `01_fsr_contact_shaping*.txt` and `02_micro_push_day1_5s_8ul.txt` unloaded until the new sensor is validated. First pilot (28 Sep) and shaping attempts showed contact activation was too rare for contingency learning.
- **Ledex `195224-230` + PVA 1.6 (0–10 V) commissioning**, following `MAGNET_COMMISSIONING.md`: Checkpoints 1–3 done (unpowered preflight: H–G ~105 kΩ, coil ~21 Ω, no case short, supply 12 V; 1 A ESKA fuse fitted in the TC-R3-32AA1 holder; power side wired D = +12 V, F = ground; first power-up 11.91 V, LED red; command 22 → H, 55 → G twisted; ao0 steps 0/0.5/1/2 V reproduced across H–G with Enable J open and coil disconnected). Imin (trimmer 4) and Imax (trimmer 3) turned ~30 turns counterclockwise (direction to be verified by current). Details: the five front-panel trimmers are, assumed left to right from the datasheet legend, rising ramp, falling ramp, Imax, Imin, dither (the mapping and the minimum direction are not stated in the datasheet; confirm with measured current at 0 V command; if Imin is actually high, Enable at 0 V would give up to ~500 mA, which the 1 A fuse tolerates but do not leave it energised). The holder lead is a stiff pre-tinned wire; where a terminal was too small a thinner wire was soldered or crimped on downstream of the fuse, with the fuse removed while wiring. Pre-power resistance checks passed (D–F no short; + to D and − to F about 0 Ω with the fuse in; fuse out gives OL). Measure `H–G` resistance only with the SCB-68A end disconnected and the DAQ idle: a live `ao0` makes the Ω reading fluctuate (stable 105 kΩ when disconnected). `ao0` was set to 0 V from NI-MAX Test Panels (Analog Output tab, `Dev1/ao0`, never `ao1`) and read ~0 V at terminals 22/55 before wiring `H/G`. Fluke for Checkpoint 4: red lead in the 10 A socket, dial on A, DC; move the lead back to `VΩ` afterwards (volts with the lead in the 10 A socket is a short). Enable: wire from `J` with a clip to clamp on the `D` screw to enable and unclip to disable. Step 4.1 DONE 11 Oct ~16:08 (Ledex wired C -> Fluke 10 A (DC) -> coil -> A; Enable clip on J hanging free; unpowered); step 4.2 DONE 11 Oct ~16:11 (powered with J open and coil connected: LED red, Fluke 0.001-0.002 A = meter offset, nothing warm); step 4.3 DONE 11 Oct ~16:16 (Enable clipped to D, LED green, current at 0 V command stayed at most 0.006 A, so Imin is effectively zero and counterclockwise is minimum on trimmer 4); step 4.4 round 1 DONE 11 Oct ~16:22 (command set with Enable open, then the clip put on D for ~2 s, unclipped, back to 0 V, 10 s off): 0.5 V -> 0.053 A, 1 V -> 0.074 A, 2 V -> 0.106 A (Fluke DC, 10 A range; sound, heat and release not yet reported). Rise is not proportional to zero (0 V gave <= 0.006 A); treat the curve as measured, not assumed. Round 1 answers: no sound, current returned to ~0 at 0 V, nothing warm. Round 2 DONE 11 Oct ~16:25: 3 V -> 0.121 A, 4 V -> 0.147 A, 5 V -> 0.180 A (nearly linear, ~26-33 mA per volt). At 5 V this is well below the datasheet's rough 0.30 A at minimum gain, so the output is limited by the Imax gain setting, not by the 12 V supply (0.18 A x 20.7 ohm = 3.7 V). Extrapolated 10 V is ~0.32 A, so 0.58 A may be unreachable at the current Imax trimmer position. Round 3 DONE 11 Oct ~16:29: 6 V -> 0.201 A, 8 V -> 0.273 A, 10 V -> 0.310 A. Full curve (V -> A): 0 -> <=0.006, 0.5 -> 0.053, 1 -> 0.074, 2 -> 0.106, 3 -> 0.121, 4 -> 0.147, 5 -> 0.180, 6 -> 0.201, 8 -> 0.273, 10 -> 0.310 (cold, short pulses). Maximum reachable current at the current Imax setting is ~0.31 A at 10 V, about half the Ledex nominal 0.58 A; no command can exceed 0.58 A, so the maximum allowed AO command is the AO range limit of 10 V. Sound/heat for round 3 not yet reported. Round 3: no sound, no warmth, released cleanly at 0 V. CHECKPOINT 4 COMPLETE 11 Oct ~16:31. Decision: leave Imax unchanged for now (max ~0.31 A at 10 V); if force at the real air gap is too weak, raise Imax clockwise in small steps and re-measure current. Step 5.1 (safe shutdown: Enable unclipped, ao0 0 V, supply unplugged, Fluke lead back to VΩ) given 11 Oct ~16:33. Checkpoint 5 status 11 Oct ~16:35: steel target is already on the object; the Ledex holder is NOT printed yet (expected next week), so the final mount waits; the joystick with the object handle can be fitted any time; home is the side closer to the mouse (the mouse pushes the object away from itself), so the Ledex goes on the mouse side of the object and any attraction pulls the object back toward home, opposing the push. User chose a temporary clamped-Ledex test (11 Oct ~16:40). Step 5.2 given: coil rewired straight to C/A (no meter; 1 V is about 0.074 A), joystick with object handle and steel target fitted and checked for free travel and spring return, Ledex firmly clamped on the home side facing the washer along the push axis with a 15-20 mm gap, nothing steel nearby, plunger status to be reported. Next: step 5.3, pulses at 1, 2, 5, 10 V (Enable clip method, 10 s off), recording whether the object is pulled toward home, the first command that moves it, spring return, and clean release at 0 V. LabVIEW magnet-safety work (clamp, guaranteed 0 V on every exit path, logging) stays for later. 11 Oct ~16:56: the Ledex is held on white tack (borderline, add a second support), the plunger is loosely inside the body and the gap to the target is about 1-2 cm. Advice given: remove the plunger and store it far from the coil (a loose plunger can be pulled in or ejected; it would also take the pull instead of the washer), add a second support, start at 1 V. 11 Oct ~17:21 user result: with the plunger OUT no pull could be felt even at 10 V (0.31 A); with the plunger in, its tip became slightly magnetic and pulling the plunger felt much stronger, so the iron core matters. Revised advice: do not run with a loose plunger; lock it as a fixed core with its tip toward the washer, bring the gap down to about 3-5 mm, then repeat 1, 2, 5, 10 V pulses; if still weak, raise Imax in small steps (force goes roughly with current squared). 11 Oct ~17:25 user chose the fixed-core, washer-as-target option. Advice given: for testing, push the plunger fully in against its stop (the energised position, so it cannot be ejected) and tape it so it does not slide out when off; washer 3-5 mm from the coil face and plunger end; repeat 1, 2, 5, 10 V clip pulses. Unknown: whether a seated plunger gives less external field than a partly extended one (test it); final plunger position and a retainer should go into the printed holder after testing. No permanent adhesive yet. 11 Oct ~17:37 user: with the plunger fully in the tip still protrudes (good for a washer target); by finger the tip pull at 1 V vs 10 V feels minimal, but pulling the plunger itself shows a clear difference with voltage. Finger feel is not a measurement; step 5.4 given: measure the washer pull on a 0.01 g scale (washer on a non-magnetic block, coil above at a fixed plastic-spacer gap such as 3 mm, scale reading drops when the washer is pulled up; 1 g is ~9.8 mN) at 0, 1, 2, 5, 10 V with 2-3 s clip pulses and 10 s off. No measured mouse push force exists yet to compare against. 11 Oct ~18:01: hand-holding the coil made the scale useless, so the setup is inverted: Ledex on the scale with the tip up (plunger in, taped), the washer taped under a non-magnetic plank on a book bridge, gap set with a plastic shim that is slid out, scale tared with the coil off; read the drop at 0, 1, 2, 5, 10 V (clip pulses 2-3 s, 10 s off) plus the 0 V noise floor. 11 Oct ~18:25: the user has nothing to fix the washer rigidly above the scale, so the washer-pull measurement is deferred until the printed holder arrives next week and should use a printed jig (gap spacer or bridge holding the washer a fixed distance from the tip, with the washer fixed externally; a washer resting on the coil on the same scale reads no change because the force is internal). Which face of the Ledex faces the washer (plunger tip, coil end face, back) is untested; plunger type (flat or 60 deg) unknown. Datasheet facts found 11 Oct (Ledex STA 20 x 40 mm pull catalogue, and the 75 STA 195204-230 sheet with the same 20.7 ohm / 7 W / 0.58 A coil): 30 AWG, 1491 turns, 855 ampere-turns at 7 W; holding force (seated) 22.0 N flat face or 12.7 N 60 deg plunger; continuous-duty plunger pull at 7 W is about 5.9 N at 1.5 mm stroke, 3.6 N at 3.2 mm, 1.9 N at 6.4 mm, 1.1 N at 9.5 mm, 0.56 N at 12.7 mm. The Checkpoint 4 currents were measured with the plunger fully seated (user confirmed), so the relevant datasheet number is the seated holding force (22.0 N flat face or 12.7 N 60 deg plunger at 0.58 A; plunger type not yet known). Scaled by current squared from the measured currents (probably an underestimate at low current because of saturation at full power): 0.074 A (1 V) ~0.2-0.4 N, 0.106 A (2 V) ~0.4-0.7 N, 0.180 A (5 V) ~1.2-2.1 N, 0.310 A (10 V) ~3.6-6.3 N. These are forces holding the plunger in the coil only; Ledex publishes nothing for an external washer at the plunger tip, so that force must be measured. Alternative (plunger linked to the object) conflicts with the contact-free 1D design and is not preferred. Open design question for later: with the plunger out the coil is a weaker electromagnet; decide after the test whether the final design keeps a fixed plunger as a pole piece, changes the gap, or raises Imax. Step 4.1 was: wire the Ledex to C/A with the DMM in series (10 A range) and a removable Enable lead to J, all unpowered, steel target away. PVA outside the mouse box, command wires 30–100 cm twisted, coil cable 1–2 m twisted and away from `ai0/ai1/ai2`. Short pulses, long cool-downs; stop on heat, smell, binding, wrong direction or failure to release. Endpoint is a repeatable low-duty pull at a measured current, not force calibration. Terminal map: D +12 V, F power ground, J Enable, H/G command, C/A coil. Random-force sessions need this proportional driver (not a binary MOSFET switch). Spare: Bürkert 316532 (due 2 Dec) if not cancelled.
- **Spout at Run:** set `Session start spout (V)` = 3.0 V before Run. Day-one file holds extend and retract at 3.00 V, so the spout stays extended. Normal Stop retracts to 0 V.
- **Home voltage depends on whether the shaft and object are fitted.** Remeasure before loading any file. For the current object-on setup use `parameters/training/02_micro_push_day1_5s_8ul.txt` (rest ~2.25 V, home 2.225–2.350, `home TO` 1 ms, reward 0.00–2.22, move time 5000 ms, water 240 ms, 250 rows, 500 ms ITI, spout 3 V) and the FSR shaping files. `02_micro_push.txt` and stages 03–08 still use the 2.50 V / `home TO` 250 ms calibration.
- **Bench presets** (no animal): `parameters/push_bench_full-system.txt` (20 rows; home/start match `training/07_expert.txt`; success 0.00–2.22 V, no end dwell; 50 ms cue, 500 ms reward delay, spout 0→3 V, 185 ms water, 1500 ms consumption; set Session start spout to 0 V so motion is visible; `mag=0`; never use to energise the Ledex/PVA) and `push_bench_full-system_nocue.txt` (same, cue 0 ms; if a click is heard disconnect the buzzer from P0.1). Procedure in `parameters/README.md`. `parameters/push_bench_wide-zone.txt` is the older hand-test file.
